    "title": "Wall Street Mixed in Early Trading",
    "text": "• The Kospi index in South Korea plunged nearly 11% on heavy selling of computer chipmaking stocks.\n• Trading was temporarily halted at times as Kospi dropped to its lowest level since April.\n• Shares in chipmaker Samsung Electronics sank 13.4% while those of SK Hynix tumbled 14.7%.\n• Futures for the S&P 500 were unchanged, while futures for the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 0.7%.\n• Futures for the technology-heavy Nasdaq slid 1% with most chipmakers down anywhere from 2% to 5%.\n• SK Hynix’s U.S.-traded shares fell to below the $149 initial public offering price.\n• A 466% jump in the price of Chinese memory chipmaker CXMT in its trading debut underscored concerns about rising competition from Chinese AI startups.\n• Shares of Johnson & Johnson rose 3% after the drugmaker agreed to pay $5.5 billion to settle remaining lawsuits.\n• Coca-Cola Co. jumped 4.5% after it beat Wall Street's sales and profit targets.\n• United Parcel Service reported strong earnings but its shares rose a modest 1%.\n• Tokyo’s Nikkei 225 dropped 4% to 62,364.92.\n• The Taiex in Taiwan skidded 4.7%, with shares of leading chipmaker TSMC falling 3%.\n• Hong Kong's Hang Seng gained 0.4% to 25,310.85.\n• The Shanghai Composite index lost 1.2% to 3,813.31.\n• Australia's S&P/ASX 200 gained 0.6% to 8,947.80.\n• India's Sensex edged 0.1% lower.\n• Oil prices extended their declines as the U.S. and Iran refrained from strikes.\n• Brent crude fell $1.35 to $84.52 a barrel.\n• U.S. benchmark crude oil lost 81 cents to $81.80 a barrel.",
    "summary": "Wall Street is mixed in early trading Tuesday with chipmakers getting hit again after a huge sell-off in South Korea. South Korea’s Kospi index plunged nearly 11% Tuesday on heavy selling of computer chipmaking stocks that have been battered recently by fears that the AI boom may turn out to be a bubble. Tuesday’s chip stock sell-off also followed a report in the technology news publication The Information that China has begun mass production of homegrown deep ultraviolet, or DUV, chipmaking tools.",
